From: George Dunlap <gwd@xenproject.org>
Subject: x86/pv: map each vCPU's root page table in its per-domain area

For XPTI enabled VMs, restore_all_guest() copies most of the active L4
pagetable into the per-CPU root_pgt.  The L4 is read via the
directmap.

Unfortunately, there was no accounting for the fact that PV guests
have short directmaps.  For an L4 living beyond the 5T boundary (3.5T
for CONFIG_BIGMEM), Xen will copy arbitrary data from guest-controlled
virtual address space into the per-CPU root_pgt, instead of the
intended L4.

Xen has per-domain mappings for the LDT and GDT, with two pages
reserved for Xen's use in the GDT but only one in use.  Reuse GDT page
15 to map the effective L4.  The GDT limit keeps implicit accesses out
of this page.

We update this mapping in make_cr3(), the only place a non-empty value
of v->arch.cr3 is written into a guest's vcpu struct, for two reasons.
First is reference counting: this makes it easier to argue that the
reference for v->arch.cr3 itself covers the reference for the mapping.

Secondly, this guarantees that all updates, even updates made in
shadow mode, result in an update to the mapping.  During live
migration, it's possible for guest_get_eff_kern_l1e() to result in a
modified value of v->arch.cr3 (sketch below); placing the mapping
update at make_cr3() catches this, while other candidate locations
like write_ptbase() and toggle_guest_mode() don't.

Note that we introduce the idea of an "effective L4" here, to mean
"guest L4 after any transformations": XPTI copies either the actual
guest L4 if not shadowed, or the shadow L4 (transformed) if shadowed.

Sketch: guest_get_eff_kern_l1e() has a pair of toggle_guest_pt() calls
which happen if the guest is in user mode.  During live migration, a
log-dirty clean will do a domain_pause and then call
shadow_blow_tables(), unpinning the shadow of the guest user root
pagetable.  After execution resumes, the first toggle_guest_pt() could
then cause that page to become unshadowed.  The second
toggle_guest_pt() would then re-shadow it, possibly with a different
backing shadow page.

This is XSA-516 / CVE-2026-79609.

Fixes: 5784de3e2067 ("x86: Meltdown band-aid against malicious 64-bit PV guests")
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
Signed-off-by: George Dunlap <gwd@xenproject.org>
Reviewed-by: Jan Beulich <jbeulich@suse.com>
---

Changes in v4:
- Reworded the pv_unmap_eff_root_pt() comment and the lifetime note
  below.  The window has exactly v->arch.cr3's validity: in shadow mode
  both name the top-level shadow after shadow_teardown() has released
  it, until vcpu_destroy_pagetables() clears them, and nothing uses
  either by then.  The earlier wording claimed more.
- Tweaked commit message wording re sketch to make it clear it's not a race
- Reworked "last page of GDT" comment to be more technically accurate

(A patch marked v3 was sent erroneously.)

Changes in v2 (of security@ posting):
- Merged is_pv_domain() condition in make_cr3()
- Use KB(64) as requested during review
- BUILD_BUG_ON() now expressed with LAST_RESERVED_GDT_BYTE as
  requested during review
- Switched to "PV_EFF_ROOT_PT" to specify that this is after potential
  shadow transformations
- Trimmed down the commit message, clarified make_cr3() rationale.

Relative to v5:
- The window is the spare GDT page after Xen's reserved range rather
  than a new sub-area: no allocation (v2's array drew a 32x
  over-allocation remark) and nothing to create or tear down per vCPU.
- The refresh is in make_cr3(), which every root change passes through,
  shadow mode included, instead of write_ptbase() plus the toggle.
- The window is cleared in vcpu_destroy_pagetables() alongside
  v->arch.cr3, so it has exactly v->arch.cr3's validity.
- The entry.S computation keeps the v2 shape; the mapping stays
  read-only, as suggested on v2.
- Build-time checks pin the window to the reserved range.
It applies on its own to current staging, using the per-vCPU GDT L1
table Xen already keeps.
